WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.533
<v Leo>Okay, the big question.

00:00:02.553 --> 00:00:04.302
<v Leo>Who actually clicks approve?

00:00:04.322 --> 00:00:05.884
<v Ava>The approval chain decides.

00:00:05.904 --> 00:00:09.144
<v Ava>It's the route an item takes, one sign-off at a time.

00:00:09.794 --> 00:00:13.288
<v Ava>There are five default chains: global policy, process,

00:00:13.308 --> 00:00:17.693
<v Ava>legal obligation, public company controls and project documents.

00:00:17.713 --> 00:00:20.313
<v Leo>Wait, can I create my own approval chain?

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:20.941
<v Ava>Yes.

00:00:20.961 --> 00:00:24.789
<v Ava>Editors can change any default chain or build a brand new one.

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:29.964
<v Ava>Open Approvals, edit a chain or click New chain, and name each step clearly.

00:00:29.984 --> 00:00:31.477
<v Leo>Then who signs each step?

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:32.906
<v Leo>Can I just put Chip?

00:00:32.926 --> 00:00:34.169
<v Ava>Chip has no hands.

00:00:34.189 --> 00:00:37.603
<v Ava>Assign named people, then choose whether any or all must sign.

00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:41.923
<v Leo>So if my approver's on vacation, I add a backup and set it to any?

00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:42.766
<v Ava>Exactly.

00:00:42.786 --> 00:00:45.993
<v Ava>Mark steps optional too, like works council consultation,

00:00:46.013 --> 00:00:48.797
<v Ava>and skip them with a note where not needed.

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:51.903
<v Ava>The owner clicks Submit for approval.

00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:54.836
<v Ava>Each approver, in turn, sees it under Waiting on you.

00:00:54.856 --> 00:00:57.598
<v Leo>So this is where someone actually clicks approve.

00:00:57.618 --> 00:00:58.338
<v Ava>Right here.

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:02.056
<v Ava>Approve, or send it back with a reason the owner can act on.

00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:04.993
<v Leo>And if Legal sends it back?

00:01:05.013 --> 00:01:09.802
<v Ava>The owner fixes it and resubmits, and the chain carries on to final approval.

00:01:12.852 --> 00:01:15.876
<v Ava>Every approval, send-back and reason is time-stamped.

00:01:15.896 --> 00:01:17.780
<v Ava>That audit trail is your evidence.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.697
<v Leo>So when auditors ask who approved it, nobody digs through email.

00:01:21.717 --> 00:01:22.966
<v Leo>Chip's thrilled.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:26.758
<v Ava>Put named approvers on every chain by October 30,

00:01:26.778 --> 00:01:29.515
<v Ava>and run one test submission end to end.

00:01:29.535 --> 00:01:32.778
<v Leo>Next time, a policy goes live in 13 countries.

00:01:32.798 --> 00:01:34.284
<v Leo>Bring snacks.
